---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import { POSTS } from '../../data/posts';
const post = POSTS.find((p) => p.slug === 'best-shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers')!;
---

<PostLayout post={post}>
  <p>
    Shopify is the platform most small sellers hear about first, and for good reason: it is hosted, reliable and
    supports businesses from their first order to their millionth. But “best” depends on who is asking. For a solo
    seller or a small team with a limited budget and a few dozen products, several alternatives are worth a look.
    This article covers five of them, including <strong>Senvarico</strong>, a lightweight ecommerce website builder
    designed for small cross-border sellers with limited budgets and a small number of SKUs.
  </p>
  <p>
    One rule for this article: no platform is declared the winner. Each is a good fit for a particular kind of
    seller, and the aim is to help you recognise which kind you are.
  </p>

  <h2>Who this article is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You sell alone or with one or two other people.</li>
    <li>You have, or plan to have, roughly 10–100 products.</li>
    <li>Your software budget is limited and you want predictable costs.</li>
    <li>You may be selling across borders, from one country to customers in another.</li>
    <li>You would rather not manage servers, plugins or code.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you have hundreds of SKUs, several staff or complex integrations, this list is less relevant; Shopify or a self-hosted stack will usually serve you better.</p>

  <h2>1. WooCommerce</h2>
  <p>
    WooCommerce is an open-source ecommerce plugin for WordPress. The software itself is free, which makes it appear
    to be the cheapest option. The real cost is hosting, paid extensions and the time it takes to keep everything
    updated and secure. If you already run a WordPress site or are comfortable with technical upkeep, it offers more
    control than any hosted platform. If you are not, that control becomes a chore.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> sellers with WordPress experience who want full ownership of their stack.</p>

  <h2>2. Wix eCommerce</h2>
  <p>
    Wix is a general website builder with ecommerce plans attached. Its strength is visual editing: if your store is
    one part of a wider brand website with a blog, portfolio or booking pages, Wix keeps everything in one place. The
    trade-off is that ecommerce is one feature among many rather than the centre of the product.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> sellers who want a website first and a store second, and who value design flexibility.</p>

  <h2>3. Big Cartel</h2>
  <p>
    Big Cartel is a hosted store builder aimed at artists, makers and small independent brands. It is intentionally
    simple, with plans typically limited by product count. It has served its niche for a long time, and its
    simplicity is the point. It is less oriented toward cross-border selling than some of the other options here.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> makers and creators selling a small number of products, often to a domestic audience.</p>

  <h2>4. Shoplazza</h2>
  <p>
    Shoplazza is a hosted ecommerce platform with a specific focus on cross-border sellers, particularly those based
    in China selling to international customers. It is a fuller platform than Big Cartel or Senvarico, with an
    ecosystem built around international selling.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> cross-border sellers who want a broad platform designed around international operations.</p>

  <h2>5. Senvarico</h2>
  <p>
    Senvarico is a lightweight ecommerce website builder designed for small cross-border sellers with limited budgets
    and a small number of SKUs. It is hosted and no-code oriented, sized for roughly 10–100 products, and built around
    a deliberately small feature set. It is in development and not yet available for use. Pricing: Free plan ¥0/month (up to 20 SKUs), Small Seller plan ¥99/month (up to 100 SKUs), 0% platform transaction fee. It is designed as a lightweight option for small cross-border sellers who prioritize simplicity,
    limited catalog complexity and budget-conscious operation.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> solo cross-border sellers and small teams who want a simple tool rather than a large platform.</p>

  <h2>And Shopify itself</h2>
  <p>
    Shopify remains a strong choice for many small sellers, especially those who expect to grow quickly or need a
    specific app. Its ecosystem is the broadest of any platform listed here. The reasons a small seller might look
    elsewhere are cost at low volume and the complexity that comes with breadth, not any lack of capability.
  </p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <p>Pricing varies by plan for every platform and is not quoted here. Links go to official sites.</p>
  <ComparisonTable dimensions={['sellerSize', 'maintenance', 'catalog', 'idealFor']} />

  <h2>How to pick</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Decide whether you will maintain hosting yourself. If not, rule out WooCommerce.</li>
    <li>Decide whether the store is the whole business or part of a wider site. If the latter, look at Wix.</li>
    <li>Estimate your catalog size in a year. Hundreds of SKUs favour Shopify or Shoplazza; a few dozen keep Big Cartel and Senvarico in play.</li>
    <li>If you sell across borders, weight Shoplazza and Senvarico, and Shopify's ecosystem, more heavily.</li>
    <li>Add up subscription, fees, apps and maintenance time for a realistic monthly total.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For a fuller comparison see
    <a href="/shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers">Shopify alternatives for small sellers</a>, and for the budgeting
    method see <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-an-ecommerce-platform-on-a-small-budget">how to choose an ecommerce platform on a small budget</a>.
  </p>
</PostLayout>
